This package installs gpclient and gpauth with embedded webview authentication
disabled. It does not install WebKitGTK, a graphical client, gpservice, or a
system service. Run the connection when needed; OpenRC is supported.

Authenticate in your desktop browser as your normal user, capturing gpauth's
JSON output in a private file. Then pass that file to gpclient as root using
--cookie-on-stdin. Replace vpn.example.org with your organisation's portal.

    gpauth vpn.example.org --browser default
    gpclient connect vpn.example.org --cookie-on-stdin

These are the underlying commands, not a complete copy-and-paste sequence.
See the overlay README for secure file-transfer instructions between terminals:

    https://github.com/dimpase/globalprotect-overlay#connect-with-an-external-browser

The JSON output and callback URL contain credentials. Do not display or share
them, or put them in shell history. Allow the browser to open the supplied
GlobalProtect browser login callback handler when prompted.

Keep the VPN terminal open. Ctrl+C disconnects; alternatively, in another root
terminal run:

    gpclient disconnect --wait 5

For automatic external-browser login, authenticate as your desktop user with
gpauth, then pass its result to gpclient as root using --cookie-on-stdin.
The supplied hidden desktop entry handles globalprotectcallback URLs.

On OpenRC/dhcpcd systems, net-dns/openresolv coordinates VPN and Wi-Fi DNS.
If resolvconf reports a signature mismatch, run resolvconf -u as root.
Disconnect before suspending the laptop; automatic suspend handling is not
configured by this package.

The package bundles the upstream modified OpenConnect sources because 3.4.0
uses APIs not provided by the stock Gentoo OpenConnect package. GnuTLS, GMP,
nettle, libxml2, lz4, p11-kit and zlib are linked from the system. The bundled
vpnc-script configures and restores routes and DNS for the VPN connection.

Source: https://github.com/yuezk/GlobalProtect-openconnect/releases/tag/v3.4.0
